{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"plinq ordering plinq ordering","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md","fileName":"PLINQ Ordering.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"PLINQ Ordering","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Data Parallelism and PLINQ]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Data Parallelism and PLINQ","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"plinq ordering plinq ordering","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md","fileName":"PLINQ Ordering.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"PLINQ Ordering","heading":"PLINQ Ordering","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"PLINQ normally favors parallel throughput over preserving a source's encounter order. `AsOrdered` requests order-sensitive results, and `AsUnordered` can remove that requirement after the portion of a query that depends on it.\n\nOrdering constrains how partitions are merged and may delay results while earlier elements finish. A query should preserve order only when it contributes to its meaning, because requesting it for convenience can erase much of the benefit of parallel execution.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Data Parallelism and PLINQ","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"plinq ordering plinq ordering","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/PLINQ Ordering.md",